Cross-File Generic Helpers
==========================

This file is the **helper** half of the cross-file generics pins.
It declares the same module path as the two test files next to it
(`generic_fn_sibling_import.lsysl`,
`generic_alias_cross_file.lsysl`) and exposes no `#test` decls of
its own.

Both companion files call into these names **without an explicit
`import` clause** — sibling files of one module must see each
other's generic functions, generic structs, and generic type
aliases directly.

The shapes here are deliberately the same as the in-file generic
coverage (`generic_fn_basic.lsysl`, `generic_struct.lsysl`,
`generic_alias_basic.lsysl`) — the test files re-pin every shape
across a file boundary, so a regression in cross-file generic
linking shows up next to the specific surface that broke.

File-local prefix `Gh` (Generic Helpers).

    module sysl.tests.lang_features.generics.siblings


### Generic function — single type parameter, identity

    gh_id[T](x: T) -> T = x


### Generic function — single type parameter, body call

`gh_double` doubles a numeric value. Only used at concrete int
instantiations by the test files. Sysl has no `Numeric` trait
constraint, so the body works for any `T` that supports `+`; the
test sites pin `int`.

    gh_double[T](x: T) -> T = x + x


### Generic function — two type parameters, first-projection

Pinned at three concrete instantiations across the test files to
exercise two-parameter inference and explicit args.

    gh_first[A, B](a: A, b: B) -> A = a


### Generic struct — single type parameter

The classic Box. Used both as a return type (so the test file
imports a `GhBox[int]` instance back) and constructed directly in
the test file.

    struct GhBox[T]
        value: T


### Generic struct — two type parameters

    struct GhPair[A, B]
        first: A
        second: B


### Generic function returning a generic struct instance

Regression-relevant: the test file's call site has to receive a
`GhBox[int]` back and read its `value` field. Cross-file generic
linking (`linkImportedGenericStructToTemplate` at sysl@4f1f81725)
is what makes `wrap(5).value` typecheck at the call site.

    gh_wrap[T](x: T) -> GhBox[T] = GhBox(x)


### Generic function whose body calls another generic function

Pins the same path as the regression — an outer generic body
referencing another generic in the same module — but at one extra
level of indirection. The outer is itself generic, so the inner's
instantiation flows from the outer's type argument.

    gh_wrap_twice[T](x: T) -> GhBox[T] = gh_wrap[T](gh_id[T](x))


### Parameterless generic-returning function

The exact shape that surfaced the sysl@4f1f81725 bug: declared
parameterless (no parens), returning a *concrete* instantiation
of a generic struct. The test file's bare `gh_zero_box` reference
must auto-call across files — if the parameterless flag is
dropped on the wire, the cross-file caller resolves it as a
function pointer and inference breaks.

    gh_zero_box -> GhBox[int] = GhBox(0)


### Generic type alias — function-typed, single type parameter

The shape `(T) -> T`. Test file uses it at `GhUnary[int]` as a
function parameter type.

    type GhUnary[T] = (T) -> T


### Generic type alias — two type parameters, function-typed

Two-parameter alias whose body is `(A, A) -> B`. Test file uses
it at `GhBin[int, int]`.

    type GhBin[A, B] = (A, A) -> B


### Generic type alias — newtype-shaped (alias body = type variable)

`GhIdAlias[T]` is just `T` — the simplest possible alias body.

    type GhIdAlias[T] = T


### Functions that consume the generic aliases at concrete instantiations

These both serve as call sites for the test file: by calling them
from the test, we exercise that the alias name itself resolves
across the file boundary (parser + analyzer), and that the
function dispatches correctly at runtime.

    gh_apply_unary(f: GhUnary[int], x: int) -> int = f(x)

    gh_apply_bin(f: GhBin[int, int], a: int, b: int) -> int = f(a, b)
